<speak>
<p>Let u be an odd integer greater than one. Our polynomial has Gaussian integer coefficients, and its product with its star equals u squared in the cyclic group ring. We can substitute any u squaredth root of unity for P, because these inputs respect P to the u squared equals one. When u is three, there are nine allowed inputs on the unit circle. We want the distance of each output from the origin.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Write f of P as the sum of a sub j times P to the j. Star conjugates each coefficient and reverses each power. The term a sub j times P to the j becomes the conjugate of a sub j times P to the negative j. Both changes matter when the coefficients are Gaussian integers.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Now substitute a root of unity, zeta. Its inverse equals its complex conjugate. So the starred term is the conjugate of the whole evaluated term, a sub j times zeta to the j. In the complex plane, this reflects the term's arrow across the real axis. Add the terms: f star at zeta equals the conjugate of f at zeta. Polynomial star and complex conjugation agree after this substitution.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Evaluate the norm identity. The output times its complex conjugate equals u squared. This is its squared absolute value, so its absolute value is u. Every output is nonzero and lies on the circle of radius u. Its angle may change with the input; its distance from the origin stays fixed.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>For a concrete example, let u equal three and f of P equal three i P. Its star is negative three i P inverse, so their product is nine. This is a Gaussian polynomial example, not a Hadamard sign row.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Send the nine input roots through this polynomial. Multiplication by three i turns each input counterclockwise by ninety degrees and scales its magnitude by three. The outputs lie on the radius three circle. The input roots and output values occupy different planes.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Compare f of one with f of omega, where omega is e to the two pi i over three. Divide the numerator by the denominator. Their magnitudes cancel, leaving a point on a separate unit circle. The phase is the numerator's angle minus the denominator's: the rotation from f of omega to f of one. Here it is clockwise one hundred twenty degrees, giving omega squared. Equal magnitudes alone do not limit this rotation to finitely many angles. We study that extra arithmetic restriction next.<break time="900ms"/></p>
</speak>
